#{[17592186421624 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421624 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421624 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421624 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{64}∼N(-5,{16}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $n=64$ from a normal population, what is the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421624 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "852d80be-f1db-58b0-9277-e24910a8ce62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421624 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=64,\\,\\mu =-5,\\,\\sigma =16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(-5,\\frac{{16}^{2}}{64}) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421625 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421625 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421625 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421625 #edb/ref 1071 "The systolic blood pressure of adults in a given population is normally distributed with a mean of $123.4\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and a standard deviation of $19.5\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$ Suppose $10$ adults are randomly selected. What is the probability that the mean systolic blood pressure for this sample will be between $120\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and $125\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}?$ \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. * \n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2946\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2912\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2877\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3300\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3264\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3228\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3669\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3632\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3594\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th><p></p>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5160\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5199\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5239\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.1\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5557\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5596\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5636\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5948\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5987\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6026\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421625 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a16286a3-509a-570e-8f6f-6720e9566628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421625 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the population mean $\\mu =123.4,$ the population standard deviation $\\sigma =19.5,$ and that the sample size $n=10.$ Therefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(123.4,\\frac{{19.5}^{2}}{10}) \\\\  & ∼N(123.4,38.025).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(120<\\overset{―}{X}<125).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(120<\\overset{―}{X}<125) & =P(\\frac{120-123.4}{\\sqrt{38.025}}<Z<\\frac{125-123.4}{\\sqrt{38.025}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.55<Z<0.26) \\\\  & =Φ(0.26)-Φ(-0.55)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.55) & =0.2912 \\\\ Φ(0.26) & =0.6026.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(120<\\overset{―}{X}<125) & =Φ(0.26)-Φ(-0.55) \\\\  & ≈0.6026-0.2912 \\\\  & =0.3114.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421626 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421626 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421626 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421626 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{24}∼N(5,{12}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $n=24$ from a normal population, what is the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421626 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a99bfe3d-da14-5bbc-aa4c-d4eaa279c627" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421626 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=24,\\,\\mu =5,\\,\\sigma =12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(5,\\frac{{12}^{2}}{24}) \\\\  & ∼N(5,6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421627 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421627 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421627 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421627 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{27}∼N(2,{9}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $27$ from a normal population, calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>3).$\n\n*Hint: A value table of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ | $\\,0.7224\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ | $\\,0.7549\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ | $\\,0.7852\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421627 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bab81480-9192-56c0-939c-2fac0be1b0c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421627 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(2,\\frac{{9}^{2}}{27}) \\\\  & ∼N(2,3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>3).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>3) & =P(Z>\\frac{3-2}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>0.58).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>0.58) & =1-P(Z\\le 0.58) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.58).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.58)=0.7190.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>3) & =1-Φ(0.58) \\\\  & =1-0.7190 \\\\  & =0.2810.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421628 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421628 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421628 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421628 #edb/ref 1071 "The volumes of soda in cans filled in at a particular factory are normally distributed with a mean of $12\\,\\text{oz}$ and a standard deviation of $0.4\\,\\text{oz}.$ Suppose $100$ cans are randomly selected. What is the probability that the mean volume for this sample will be more than $12.1\\,\\text{oz}?$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9938\\,$ | $\\,0.9940\\,$ | $\\,0.9941\\,$ | $\\,0.9943\\,$ | $\\,0.9945\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9953\\,$ | $\\,0.9955\\,$ | $\\,0.9956\\,$ | $\\,0.9957\\,$ | $\\,0.9959\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9965\\,$ | $\\,0.9966\\,$ | $\\,0.9967\\,$ | $\\,0.9968\\,$ | $\\,0.9969\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421628 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf44009c-d977-5a1b-baed-2f30870bd66c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421628 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the population mean $\\mu =12,$ the population standard deviation $\\sigma =0.4,$ and that the sample size $n=100.$ Therefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(12,\\frac{{0.4}^{2}}{100}) \\\\  & ∼N(12,0.0016).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>12.1).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>12.1) & =P(Z>\\frac{12.1-12}{\\sqrt{0.0016}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>2.5) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<2.5) \\\\  & =1-Φ(2.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(2.5)=0.9938.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>12.1) & =1-Φ(2.5) \\\\  & =1-0.9938 \\\\  & =0.0062.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421629 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421629 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143263" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421629 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421629 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}∼N(12,{6}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $n=18$ from a normal population, what is the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421629 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc9af1e1-2430-571a-97fc-932c8c2c6f9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421629 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=18,\\